<article class="env-lemma" id="08IX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08IX">Lemma <span data-tag="08IX">110.48.2</span>.</a> There exists a flat group scheme of finite type over the infinite dimensional affine space which is not quasi-separated. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The same construction as above can be carried out with the infinite dimensional affine space $S = \mathbf{A}^\infty _ k = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}k[x_1, x_2, \ldots ]$ as the base and the origin $0 \in S$ corresponding to the maximal ideal $(x_1, x_2, \ldots )$ as the closed point which is doubled in $G$. The resulting group scheme $G \rightarrow S$ is not quasi-separated as explained in Schemes, Example <a href="/tag/01KL" data-tag="01KL">26.21.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>